From d871e9711ecee1a34d6690fdc1fd2b623f3f2a6e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 22:23:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Fix the first-run blocker, and record before-images on
 register updates
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two findings from using Meridian to run a real delivery programme — five
projects, seventeen stages, four gates each, nine RAID items — over a
working day.

1. Following README.md exactly produces an empty instance.

   The README says: "With no DATABASE_URL the server runs PGlite from
   server/.data/pgdata". It does not. `.env.example` sets PGLITE_DIR, but
   nothing loads `.env` — dotenv is not a dependency and is imported
   nowhere — so PGLITE_DIR is unset and the fallback is an in-memory
   database.

   The failure is silent and total. `npm run seed` reports "seeded 12
   projects · 10 users" and that database dies with the process; `npm run
   dev` opens a fresh empty one; the sign-in screen offers no accounts
   (/api/auth/accounts returns seeded:false) and every documented password
   is refused. Nothing in the output suggests what happened.

   Fixed by making the code do what the README says: the default data
   directory is server/.data/pgdata, resolved from db.js rather than from
   the working directory so all three commands agree wherever they are run
   from. An explicitly passed dataDir still wins, including `null` — that
   is how view-render and usecase-sweep ask for a throwaway in-memory
   instance, and they must keep getting one or a sweep would overwrite the
   real book.

   Also: PGlite creates the last path element only, so a data directory
   whose parent is missing fails with ENOENT before it opens. mkdir -p
   first.

2. Closing a register item recorded no before-image.

   audit_event carries before_json/after_json and record() writes them, but
   among the audited() call sites they are passed on deletions and on a few
   exceptional acts (gate override, re-baseline) — not on ordinary updates.
   Over 181 audit rows written by the campaign above, none carried a
   before-image, including eight "Item closed" rows.

   That is the wrong way round for a register: items are closed constantly
   and deleted almost never, and "who closed this risk, and what did it say
   before?" is the first question asked at review. The trail could say a
   risk was closed and by whom, but not what it had been.

   PATCH /api/raid/:id now passes the prior status, title, detail,
   probability, impact, response and owner, plus the patch as the after-
   image. The same gap remains on other update routes and is worth the same
   treatment; this fixes the one that matters most.

Verified: 449 tests pass, the nine audit gates pass, the build passes, and
a clean clone now reaches a signed-in session with `npm install && npm run
seed && npm run dev` and no environment variables.

