Two improvements from an evaluation that used Meridian to govern a real
delivery programme end to end. Both were named by this codebase before they
were built.

── INT-10 · the bridge ───────────────────────────────────────────────

The evaluation's largest objection: Meridian is not where the work happens
and had no bridge to where it does, so every stage completion was recorded
twice — once in the engineering repository, once here. Re-entered status is
stale status, and that is how a PMO tool stops being kept up to date, then
stops being believed.

v1.js already named the resolution — "les écritures viendront avec les
intégrations qui les demandent, jamais avant" — and integrations.js named
this exact item, INT-10 (Jira, Azure DevOps). This is that integration.

No Jira connector. No Azure DevOps connector. The interoperability
committee's "four surfaces instead of twenty connectors" was right: twenty
connectors are twenty authentication debts, twenty formats that change
without warning, and twenty reasons not to upgrade. What is added is ONE
generic surface that knows the name of no tool.

Three refusals make it safe:

- A key cannot LINK. Attaching an external item to a stage is a session act
  under the project's schedule.write. A stolen key cannot hang invented work
  on any stage in a portfolio, nor decide a project is finished. It can say
  only "this ticket, which somebody already attached here, is closed".
- A key cannot write a PERCENTAGE. It reports item states; the stage's
  percent becomes closed over linked. A system that could write "78%" makes
  the number unverifiable, whereas "7 of 9" can be re-read, disputed and
  traced to objects an auditor can go and look at. A pct in the request body
  is ignored, and a test shows it.
- Nothing is SILENT. Unknown items return in `unlinked` so an integrator can
  see what still needs mapping rather than believing everything landed. A
  stage a person edited between the read and the write returns in
  `contended` and the next report picks it up — refusing a whole batch over
  one row would be absurd, and overwriting it silently is exactly what
  row_version exists to prevent.

Every movement is audited under the integration's NAME, which is what
migration 025 asked for: "« système » n'est pas une réponse à « qui a écrit
cette ligne »".

One drift was found while building it: the discovery endpoint listed its
endpoints from a hand-kept array and so announced two doors on the day there
were three. It now derives them from the OpenAPI description, which was
already the source of truth for the contract.

── Before-images on every update route ───────────────────────────────

The first patch fixed PATCH /api/raid/:id by hand and said the gap remained
elsewhere. This closes it in two places rather than forty.

updateVersioned() now captures the row in the SAME statement that changes
it — a FROM subquery evaluated against the snapshot at statement start, so
the image cannot disagree with what was actually updated, and it costs no
extra round trip. audited() fills it in when the call site did not name its
own. Every update route records a before-image now, including the ones
nobody thought to annotate.

It rides back on a Symbol, so the two routes that return that object
straight to the client did not start serialising whole rows. And
`t.row_version + 1` is qualified, because `b` carries a column of the same
name and Postgres calls the bare reference ambiguous — which is how the
first attempt failed.

── Verified ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

468 tests pass (was 449), the nine static gates pass, and the build passes.
Two existing tests pin the exact scope list and migration set and were
updated — which is the point of having them: an addition has to be
deliberate.

docs/33-pont-suivi-de-taches.md carries the contract, the three refusals,
what the bridge deliberately does NOT do, and a GitHub Actions example.

